#import "../../template.typ": *

#plant-header("Mops Mugo Pine", "Pinus mugo 'Mops'", "mugo")[
  A beautifully compact, symmetrical dwarf pine with rich dark-green needles and decorative spring 'candles.' Extraordinarily cold hardy, wind resistant, and long-lived. Perfect for rock gardens, slopes, foundation plantings, and perennial borders. Holds its shape naturally with minimal maintenance. Established 2+ years — on drip irrigation.
]

=== Watering

Established plants on drip: very drought tolerant once established. Target 2–4 gallons per watering event, once per week in summer. For 1 gph emitters, run 30–45 minutes. In dry summers, water 2–3 times per month to prevent needle browning. In clay soil, check moisture at 4-inch depth before each cycle — skip if still moist. Avoid overwatering and poorly drained locations.

=== Fertilization

If feeding at all, use a slow-release N-K evergreen formula with zero phosphorus (your soil P is HIGH). A chelated micronutrient application in spring addresses your very low Fe, Zn, and Cu, which are more limiting than macronutrients. A light application every 1–2 years is sufficient — over-fertilizing promotes excessive candle elongation and disrupts the compact form.

=== Pruning & Maintenance

To keep Mops compact, pinch new 'candles' (soft spring growth) back by one-half to two-thirds in late spring (May–June) as they are elongating. This is far preferable to shearing. Avoid cutting back into old, brown wood — pines will not regenerate from old wood. Remove dead inner needles by hand annually.

=== Wildlife Value

Mops Mugo Pine provides excellent dense shelter for house finches, which often nest within the compact canopy. The low, mounded form provides ground-level cover for cottontail rabbits in harsh weather. Birds rarely eat the small pine cones. European collared doves may perch on or near the plant. Limited direct food value, but the dense evergreen structure is valuable for winter bird shelter. Field mice may burrow near the root zone; maintain a clear 4-inch trunk zone.

=== Pests & Disease

#link("https://www.google.com/search?q=pine+needle+scale+treatment")[Pine needle scale] appears as white scales on needles; treat with horticultural oil in spring when crawlers are active (May). #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=pine+sawfly+treatment")[Pine sawfly] larvae can defoliate individual branches rapidly — remove by hand or treat with spinosad. #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=diplodia+tip+blight+mugo+pine+treatment")[Diplodia tip blight] causes shoot die-back starting at new candles in spring; if history of this disease, apply fungicide (thiophanate-methyl) during candle emergence. In Colorado's dry climate, fungal diseases are less common than in humid regions.

=== Other Care

Excellent deer resistance. Very long-lived — this is a 30–50 year plant. Inspect annually for dead interior wood and clear it out for air circulation. The compact form is naturally maintained with annual candling — do not let candles fully extend if you want a tighter, more refined shape each year.
